<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edmund Burke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1729–1797</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edmund Burke was an Irish-born statesman, orator and political thinker, educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>. As a Whig member of the British Parliament he supported conciliation with the American colonies and relief for Irish Catholics, and he led the impeachment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a>. His Reflections on the Revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> of 1790 made him a founding figure of modern conservatism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-burke.jpg" alt="Edmund Burke" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Joshua_Reynolds_-_Edmund_Burke,_1729_-_1797._Statesman,_orator_and_author_-_PG_2362_-_National_Galleries_of_Scotland_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
